        <h2>What Are AIA Pay Applications? G702 vs. G703</h2>
        <p>
          The American Institute of Architects (AIA) publishes standardized contract documents used
          across the construction industry. The two forms used for progress billing are:
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li>
            <strong>G702 — Application and Certificate for Payment.</strong> This is the cover sheet.
            It summarizes the total contract amount, work completed to date, retainage, previous payments,
            and the current amount due.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>G703 — Continuation Sheet.</strong> This is the detail. It breaks down the contract
            into line items (your schedule of values) and shows the work completed and materials stored
            for each line item.
          </li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          Together, these two forms tell the GC and owner exactly how much work you've done, how
          much you've been paid, and how much you're requesting this period. Many GCs accept a
          G702/G703-style application rather than the licensed AIA documents; check your subcontract
          before the first one goes out.
        </p>

        <h2>The Monthly Pay Application Cycle</h2>
        <p>
          A pay application is less a form than a monthly routine with deadlines set by people above
          you in the payment chain. On a typical commercial job it runs like this:
        </p>
        <ol>
          <li>
            <strong>The schedule of values is approved.</strong> Before the first application, the GC
            (and often the architect) reviews and approves your line items. Everything after this bills
            against those lines.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>You send a pencil draft.</strong> Many GCs ask for a draft — a "pencil" — several
            days before their cutoff. Their project manager walks the job, compares your percentages to
            what is in place, and marks up anything they won't support.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>You submit the final application with its backup.</strong> The agreed percentages
            go on the signed application, along with whatever the subcontract requires: lien waivers,
            stored-materials invoices, certified payroll on public work, insurance certificates.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>The GC rolls it up.</strong> Your application becomes a line in the GC's own application
            to the owner, alongside every other trade's.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>The architect or owner's representative certifies it.</strong> The certificate is
            the approval of an amount — it may be less than you applied for, and the difference is what
            you reconcile before the next cycle.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Money moves down the chain.</strong> The owner pays the GC, and the GC pays you on
            the terms your subcontract sets — pay-when-paid clauses are common — within any limits a state
            prompt payment law adds. Conditional and unconditional
            <a href="/blog/construction-lien-waivers-explained/">lien waivers</a> trade hands along the
            way.
          </li>
        </ol>
        <p>
          Then the next period starts, with this month's billed amounts carried into the "previous
          applications" column. That roll-forward is where spreadsheets break: one missed carry and
          every later application is off.
        </p>

        <h2>Setting Up Your Schedule of Values</h2>
        <p>
          The <a href="/docs/pay-apps/schedule-of-values/">schedule of values</a> (SOV) is the backbone
          of every pay application you'll submit on a project. It gets established at the start of the
          job and stays with you through the final payment. Getting it right upfront saves headaches for
          the entire project duration. If you priced the job in
          <a href="/features/construction-bidding/">construction bidding software</a>, the SOV
          should follow the bid's line items — otherwise you rebuild the contract in a spreadsheet
          every month. Our explainer on
          <a href="/blog/schedule-of-values-guide/"
            >what a schedule of values is and how it changes</a
          >
          goes deeper, and the
          <a href="/templates/schedule-of-values/">schedule of values template</a> is a blank one to start
          from.
        </p>
        <p>
          Break your contract into meaningful line items that reflect actual phases of work. A
          mechanical sub might break theirs into rough-in, underground, above-ceiling, trim-out, and
          startup/commissioning. An <a href="/solutions/electrical-contractors/"
            >electrical contractor</a
          > might separate by floor, by system (power, lighting, fire alarm, low voltage), or by phase.
        </p>
        <p>Tips for structuring your SOV:</p>
        <ul>
          <li>
            <strong>Front-load strategically, but don't overdo it.</strong> It's standard practice to
            weight early work items (mobilization, submittals, rough-in) slightly higher to improve early
            cash flow. But if your SOV is obviously front-loaded, the GC or architect will reject it.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Include a mobilization line item.</strong> This covers your costs for getting set
            up on the job — temporary facilities, initial material deliveries, and coordination.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Separate labor and materials if the contract requires it.</strong> Some owners want
            to see the split. Check your subcontract.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Make line items match your work flow.</strong> You'll be billing against these items
            monthly, so they should align with how the work actually progresses.
          </li>
        </ul>

