        <h2>How the G702 and G703 Numbers Connect</h2>
        <p>
          The G702 is a math exercise once the G703 is right. Its lines walk from the contract down
          to this period's check:
        </p>
        <ol>
          <li>
            <strong>Contract sum to date</strong> — the original contract plus the net of approved change
            orders. Pending change orders don't count until they're approved.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Total completed and stored to date</strong> — the G703 column G total, carried over
            unchanged.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Retainage</strong> — the contract rate (often 5% or 10%) applied to that cumulative
            total, not just this period. Our
            <a href="/blog/retainage-construction-guide/">retainage guide</a> covers step-downs and release.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Less previous certificates for payment</strong> — what earlier applications already
            earned, net of retainage.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Current payment due</strong> — what's left. This is the number your lien waiver for
            the period should reference.
          </li>
        </ol>
        <p>
          The template page breaks down
          <a href="/templates/aia-g702-g703/">all nine lines of a G702-style application</a> with a worked
          example; the point here is the dependency. If a G703 line is wrong, every G702 line below it
          is wrong too.
        </p>
        <p>
          On the final pay application, line items go to 100% and retainage is billed so the current
          payment due includes funds held throughout the job. That last G702 is one piece of the
          <a href="/blog/construction-project-closeout-checklist/"
            >construction project closeout checklist</a
          >
          — it typically will not be processed until punch list, closeout documents, and lien waivers
          travel with it.
        </p>

        <h2>What the G703 (Continuation Sheet) Tracks</h2>
        <p>The G703 is where you track progress on each line item. For each row:</p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Column A:</strong> Item number</li>
          <li><strong>Column B:</strong> Description of work</li>
          <li><strong>Column C:</strong> Scheduled value (from your approved SOV)</li>
          <li><strong>Column D:</strong> Work completed from previous applications</li>
          <li><strong>Column E:</strong> Work completed this period</li>
          <li><strong>Column F:</strong> Materials presently stored (not yet installed)</li>
          <li><strong>Column G:</strong> Total completed and stored to date (D + E + F)</li>
          <li><strong>Column H:</strong> Percentage complete (G / C)</li>
          <li><strong>Column I:</strong> Balance to finish (C - G)</li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          The most important thing: your numbers need to be defensible. If you bill 60% on a line
          item, you should be able to walk the GC through the job and demonstrate that 60% of that
          work is actually in place. Overbilling erodes trust and leads to disputes.
        </p>

        <h2>Common Mistakes That Delay Payment</h2>
        <ul>
          <li>
            <strong>Math errors.</strong> Sounds basic, but incorrect totals are the number one reason
            pay apps get bounced. Double-check every calculation before submitting.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Missing backup documentation.</strong> Many GCs require lien waivers, <a
              href="/blog/prevailing-wage-certified-payroll/">certified payroll</a
            >, stored materials invoices, or updated insurance certificates with each pay app. Know
            what's required and include it.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Billing for stored materials without documentation.</strong> If you're billing for
            materials stored on-site or at a warehouse, you need invoices and sometimes photos. Some contracts
            require stored materials to be insured separately.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Submitting late.</strong> Pay app deadlines are usually tied to the owner's billing
            cycle. Miss the cutoff by one day and you could wait an entire month for your money.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Not matching the GC's format.</strong> If the GC uses a specific template or requires
            submission through a portal, use it. Submitting in your own format creates extra work for
            their AP team and pushes you to the back of the line.
          </li>
        </ul>

        <h2>Tips for Faster Approval</h2>
        <ol>
          <li>
            <strong>Submit early.</strong> Don't wait until the deadline. Submit a few days ahead so there's
            time for questions without missing the cycle. If your office reviews pay apps internally before
            they go out, build that
            <a href="/docs/pay-apps/approval-workflow/">approval step</a> into your timeline.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Be conservative on percentages.</strong> It's better to bill 55% when you're at 58%
            than to bill 65% and get knocked back. Consistent, honest billing builds trust.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Communicate with the GC's project manager.</strong> A quick call before you submit
            — "I'm billing 70% on rough-in this month, does that align with what you're seeing?" — can
            prevent rejections.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Keep your change orders current.</strong> Don't let unapproved
            <a href="/features/change-orders/">construction change orders</a> pile up. Push for timely
            approval so your pay app reflects the actual contract value.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Track everything digitally.</strong> Using software to
            <a href="/features/pay-applications/">manage your pay apps</a> eliminates math errors, keeps
            a clean audit trail, and makes it easy to
            <a href="/docs/pay-apps/creating/">generate the forms</a> on demand.
          </li>
        </ol>
      </div>

